CH = dict(num=6, title="Heat Transfer in Nature", theme="Conduction, convection, radiation, breezes, water cycle",
intro="Pema and Palden sit around a fireplace in Gangtok and wonder why Kerala is warmer, why cooking pans are metal and why smoke rises. The chapter explains the three ways heat travels (conduction, convection and radiation), applies them to clothing, houses and sea/land breezes, and then links the Sun's heat to the water cycle, infiltration, groundwater and aquifers.",
big_questions=["Why do we use metal pans but clay cups?", "Why does smoke rise?", "Why does the wind blow from sea to land in the day?", "How does rainwater become groundwater?"],
blocks=[
("h2", "7.0 Why places differ in temperature"),
("p", "The Sun is the main source of heat and light for the Earth. Places near the **equator** are generally hot, and coastal places like Kerala are warm and humid, while hilly places like Sikkim are cold in winter. Cooking utensils are metal because metals are good conductors of heat."),
("h2", "7.1 Conduction of Heat"),
("steps", [("Set up (Activity 7.1)", "Attach four pins with wax at about 2 cm gaps along a 15 cm aluminium or iron strip, clamp it on a stand and label the pins I to IV (I nearest the flame)."), ("Heat", "Heat the free end with a candle or spirit lamp, under adult supervision."), ("Observe", "Pin I falls first, then II, III and IV. Heat travels along the strip and melts the wax one pin at a time.")]),
("note", "DEFINITION", "**Conduction** is the transfer of heat from the hotter part of an object to the colder part. Each particle passes heat to its neighbour but **the particles themselves do not move from their positions**. Materials that conduct heat easily are **good conductors**; those that do not are **poor conductors (insulators)**."),
("ul", ["Conduction is the **main mode of heat transfer in solids**.", "With a wood or glass strip the pins would not fall because they are poor conductors.", "Metals are used for cooking vessels; **clay and porcelain** cups are used for tea and coffee, which stay hot longer because they conduct poorly."]),
("table", ["Good conductors of heat", "Poor conductors (insulators) of heat"], [["Steel, iron, aluminium, copper and other metals", "Wood, glass, clay, porcelain, plastic, air"]]),
("h3", "Applications of poor conductors"),
("ul", ["**Woollen clothes** trap air in their pores; air is a poor conductor, so heat from the body escapes slowly and we feel warm.", "**Two thin blankets** keep us warmer than one thick one because of the air layer trapped between them.", "**Mori block, Uttarkashi (Himalayas):** walls of two wooden layers filled with cow dung and mud; wood and mud are poor conductors, so they prevent heat loss in winter.", "**Hollow bricks:** trapped air keeps houses warm in winter and cool in summer."]),
("h2", "7.2 Convection"),
("steps", [("Paper cups (Activity 7.2)", "Hang two identical inverted paper cups from the ends of a balanced stick."), ("Heat", "Place a burning candle below one cup."), ("Observe", "That cup rises. The warmed air expands, occupies more space, becomes lighter and rises.")]),
("ul", ["A partly inflated balloon kept in the Sun grows larger: air **expands on heating**.", "Smoke from burning incense rises as it is a mixture of hot gases and solid particles, warmer than surrounding air. A **smoke detector** is therefore best placed on the **ceiling**."]),
("steps", [("Liquids (Activity 7.3)", "Half-fill a beaker with water and put a grain of potassium permanganate at the centre of the base with a straw."), ("Heat from below", "Place a candle under the centre of the beaker."), ("Observe", "A coloured streak rises in the middle and comes down at the sides. Hot water at the bottom expands, becomes lighter and rises; cooler, heavier water from the sides sinks to replace it. The cycle continues until all the water is hot.")]),
("note", "DEFINITION", "**Convection** is the transfer of heat by the **actual movement of particles** of liquids and gases from one place to another. Water and air are heated by convection."),
("h3", "7.2.1 Land and sea breeze (Activity 7.4)"),
("p", "Soil and water of the same amount are put in sunlight with a thermometer in each. After 20 minutes the soil is much hotter than the water; indoors the soil also cools faster. So **land heats and cools faster than water**."),
("table", ["", "Sea breeze", "Land breeze"], [["Time", "Day", "Night"], ["Which is warmer", "Land (heats faster)", "Sea (cools slower)"], ["Air movement", "Warm air above land rises; cooler air moves from **sea to land**", "Warm air above sea rises; cooler air moves from **land to sea**"], ["Effect", "Relief from heat; houses on coasts have windows facing the sea", "Wind direction reverses at night"]]),
("p", "Large water bodies also keep nearby areas from reaching extreme temperatures, as water heats and cools slowly."),
("h2", "7.3 Radiation"),
("note", "DEFINITION", "**Radiation** is the transfer of heat directly from a hot object without needing any medium. The heat of the Sun reaches the Earth, and the warmth of a fire reaches us, by radiation. **All objects radiate heat**; a hot utensil cools by radiating heat to its surroundings."),
("ul", ["**Light-coloured clothes** reflect most heat, so they are comfortable in summer.", "**Dark-coloured clothes** absorb more heat, so they suit winter."]),
("table", ["Mode", "Medium needed?", "Particle movement", "Main occurrence", "Example"], [["Conduction", "Yes", "Particles vibrate in place, passing heat on", "Solids", "Spoon in hot tea, metal strip"], ["Convection", "Yes", "Particles actually move", "Liquids and gases", "Boiling water, land/sea breeze, rising smoke"], ["Radiation", "No", "No particles involved", "Even through vacuum/space", "Sunlight, heat from a fire"]]),
("p", "**Example of all three:** in heating water in a pan, heat goes from the flame to the pan by conduction, from the pan to the water by convection, and the warmth felt around the flame and pan is by radiation. The Himalayan **bukhari** (iron stove with chimney, flat top for cooking) uses all three."),
("h2", "7.4 Water Cycle"),
("p", "The Sun's heat drives evaporation of water from clothes, oceans, rivers and lakes. Water exists as a liquid (oceans, rivers), solid (snow, glaciers, ice sheets) and gas (water vapour)."),
("steps", [("Evaporation", "Sun heats oceans, rivers and lakes; water becomes vapour. Plants also lose water vapour by **transpiration**."), ("Condensation", "Rising vapour cools and condenses into clouds."), ("Precipitation", "Water falls as rain, snow or hail."), ("Return", "Water flows into rivers, lakes and oceans or seeps into the ground; snow in mountains melts in summer and is replenished in winter.")]),
("note", "DEFINITION", "The **water cycle** is the continuous movement of water: upward as vapour and downward by precipitation, through soil, rocks and plants, finally back to water bodies. It redistributes and replenishes water and conserves the total water on Earth."),
("p", "**Know a scientist:** Varahamihira (6th century CE, Ujjain) wrote *Brihatsamhita* and predicted seasonal rainfall using clouds, winds, and positions of stars and the moon."),
("h3", "7.4.1 Seepage of water beneath the Earth (Activity 7.5)"),
("p", "Three bottles filled with clay, sand and gravel are each given 200 mL water. **Water passes fastest through gravel, slower through sand and slowest through clay**, because spaces between gravel particles are wider."),
("note", "DEFINITION", "**Infiltration** is the seeping of surface water through soil and rocks. It is easier when spaces are wide, open and interconnected. Stored water in pore spaces is **groundwater**. The underground layers of sediments and rocks that hold it are **aquifers**. Wells and bore wells are dug into aquifers (a few metres to hundreds of metres deep)."),
("ul", ["Groundwater is not unlimited: heavy extraction for a growing population and concrete surfaces in cities that reduce infiltration deplete it.", "Remedies: **rainwater harvesting** and **recharge pits**.", "**Ice stupa (Ladakh):** in winter, stream water is piped and sprayed in cold air to freeze into a cone-shaped ice structure, which melts slowly in spring to give water for farming through summer."]),
("h2", "Likely exam questions"),
("qa", [("Name the three modes of heat transfer.", "Conduction, convection and radiation."),
("Define conduction. In which state of matter is it the main mode?", "Transfer of heat from hotter to colder part without particles moving; solids."),
("Why are cooking vessels made of metal but handles of wood/plastic?", "Metals conduct heat well; wood/plastic are poor conductors."),
("Why do two thin blankets keep us warmer than one thick one?", "Air trapped between the layers is a poor conductor and reduces heat loss."),
("Define convection with an example.", "Heat transfer by actual movement of particles of liquids and gases; water in a pan, sea breeze."),
("Why does hot air rise?", "On heating it expands, becomes lighter and rises."),
("Why should a smoke detector be on the ceiling?", "Smoke is hot and rises."),
("Explain sea breeze and land breeze.", "In the day land is warmer, so air flows from sea to land; at night sea is warmer, so air flows from land to sea."),
("Why does heat from the Sun reach us?", "By radiation, which needs no medium."),
("Why do we wear light clothes in summer and dark in winter?", "Light colours reflect heat; dark colours absorb it."),
("What is the water cycle?", "Continuous movement of water by evaporation, condensation and precipitation, returning to water bodies and the ground."),
("What are infiltration, groundwater and aquifer?", "Seeping of water into the ground; water stored in pore spaces; underground layers storing water."),
("Why do hollow bricks help in houses?", "Trapped air is a poor conductor, so houses stay cool in summer and warm in winter."),
("Where do ice cubes in a dish get heat to melt?", "From the surroundings (air, dish) by conduction, convection and radiation.")]),
("h2", "Exploratory projects"),
("ul", ["Wrap a thin paper strip around a metal rod and try to burn it while rotating: the paper does not burn because the metal conducts away the heat.", "Hang a spiral-cut paper above a candle: it spins due to rising hot air (convection).", "Visit a water-harvesting site or recharge pit and prepare an illustrated report."]),
],
takeaways=["Heat moves by **conduction** (solids, particles stay), **convection** (liquids/gases, particles move) and **radiation** (no medium).", "Metals are good conductors; wood, air, glass, clay are poor conductors, used for insulation.", "Warm fluid rises and cool fluid sinks, giving **convection currents**, **sea breeze** (day) and **land breeze** (night).", "Land heats and cools faster than water.", "The Sun's radiation drives the **water cycle**: evaporation, condensation, precipitation.", "**Infiltration** recharges **groundwater** stored in **aquifers**; conserve it by rainwater harvesting and recharge pits."])
